// logos.std.wql.source — the WQL data-source abstraction (ADR 0012-impl-seam
// §10.4): `Source<Row>` / `IntoSource<Row>`, mirroring `Iterator` /
// `IntoIterator`.
//
// WHY a fresh trait instead of reusing `Iterator`: stdlib list-comprehensions
// desugar EAGERLY to array/slice `SForEach` loops only — they do NOT iterate a
// general trait. WQL's relational tier must pull from Writ object stores and
// graph edges, so it needs its own pull-based, monomorphized (zero-cost) source
// protocol. The static backend emits `IntoSource`-driven `for`-style loops.
//
// SKELETON ONLY (Phase 1c): the traits + the `IntoSource`-for-`Source` blanket.
// Concrete sources (ArraySource / WritObjectSource / RefEdgeSource) and the
// relational adapters are deferred to the executor phase.
package logos.std.wql.source;

use logos.lang.option;   // Option<Row> — the next() result

// A pull-based row producer. `next` yields `Some(row)` until exhausted.
// Monomorphized per concrete source ⇒ zero-cost (no dynamic dispatch).
pub trait Source<Row> {
    fn next(self: &mut Self) -> Option<Row>;
}

// A value convertible into a `Source<Row>` (the binding surface a `from`
// clause / data context resolves to). Mirrors `IntoIterator`.
pub trait IntoSource<Row> {
    type Src: Source<Row>;
    fn into_source(self: Self) -> Self::Src;
}

// Every `Source` is trivially an `IntoSource` of itself (identity), exactly as
// `Iterator: IntoIterator`. This is the only blanket the skeleton commits to.
impl<Row, S: Source<Row>> IntoSource<Row> for S {
    type Src = S;
    fn into_source(self: S) -> S { return self; }
}

// ── THE HUB TRAITS (#342, ADR 0025 §11) ──────────────────────────────────────
// The relational vocabulary a structure implements to join Deem, declared by
// the query plane rather than by one of its suppliers: a Memoria family, a
// `mem` collection and a hand-written source implement the same traits.

/// The RELATIONAL projection of a map — one row per entry, in NO GUARANTEED
/// ORDER (ADR 0024 S6).
///
/// Order is a SEPARATE FACT from being a map, and conflating them is what
/// `OrderedMapSource` did: a `HashMap` answers `key == k` in O(1) and cannot
/// answer `key > k` at all, so it is a perfectly good map source and not an
/// ordered one. A source says which it is by which trait it implements, and
/// declares what it can DO with `op` members either way.
pub trait MapSource<K, V> {
    rel entry(key: K, val: V);
}

/// The RELATIONAL projection of an ordered map — the Deem plane's view of any
/// `kind ordered_map` container, in the container's own vocabulary: one row per
/// entry, in key order.
///
/// ONE trait for every map family, declared here in the hub rather than
/// per-family: a family's type is hash-named (`Hs<content-hash>`), so a query
/// written against a per-family trait could not be written by hand. Against
/// this one it can — `mapping M<S: OrderedMapSource>(m: &S) { from m e … }`
/// names no family at all, and only the INSTANTIATION site mentions a concrete
/// container. The generated family supplies the materializer.
// INDEPENDENT of MapSource, not a refinement of it: a source implements one or
// the other, and which one IS the order fact. A supertrait would oblige every
// ordered source to write both impls binding the same relation, which the rel
// binding rightly refuses — one materializer, one declaration.
pub trait OrderedMapSource<K, V> {
    rel entry(key: K, val: V);
}

/// The RELATIONAL projection of a POSITIONAL container — the Deem plane's view
/// of any `kind vector` family: one row per element, in position order.
///
/// Declared in the hub for the same reason `OrderedMapSource` is: a generated
/// family is hash-named, so a query written against a per-family trait could
/// not be written by hand. Against this one it can — the query names the
/// CLASS, and the family supplies the materializer.
pub trait PositionalSource<V> {
    rel row(pos: i64, val: V);
}
